Lesson: Project Knight Travails
Repo link: https://github.com/Kirito-exe/knight-travails/tree/main/script


﻿till now, I have set up a BFS algo
 for this project, and a function that basically 
 takes a coordinates and generates all possible coordinates, 
 which is then used by the BFS algo's queue to discover more 
 nodes, until the node which we are supposed to reach is not achieved. 

The question:
The issue I am facing is thinking as to how do 
I make my BFS algo such that it discovers the 
shortest path. BFS explores nodes in the same level
first if the said graph is unweighted(a knight's move 
in chess is always equal in distance covered so I feel
like the graph should be unweighted), SO unless there is
   some kind of distinction between the nodes, BFS will always
    search all the nodes (even if the said node is going away
     from the goal), check it's children and keep doing it until
      it reaches the goal, which turns out to be quite inefficient. 


I implemented the distance logic by giving each of these edges a weight which is
distance from the end goal, then sort it according to the lowest one in every loop iteration,
but the search still takes a lot of wrong turns, It seems that it's cuz it always chooses the
closet square(as intended) but sometimes for the best path we need to move a bit away.

is it okay to search abt how to find the shortest path using BFS.